Gilleholmen
A Paradise of the Past

by Joachim Parrow

Outside gate

I am on a small dirt road in front of a big iron gate. The gate is slightly ajar, as if someone just left in a hurry. On both sides of the road are fields with grazing cows. Please don't make me go there: I am afraid of cows. Also going back seems pointless, that's just the way I came from! The only way forward is going in through the gate.

Behind the gate I can glimpse a mound surrounded by forests, mainly pine. Perhaps the road continues beyond the gate, it is hard to tell from here.

It is one of those summer days with clear skies and very little wind. It is warm but not uncomfortably hot, and I am fine in my light clothes. There is some kind of muted birdsong, otherwise all is perfect silence.

And now I have to be completely honest with you. I do not know if I remember this, if I dream it, make it all up, or if it is a mixture. To tell the truth I am not totally sure how old I am. Am I seven years, seventeen, or seventy, or all of that at the same time? What I do know is that I want to explore Gilleholmen.
> in

> in
I go in and the gate closes behind me with a resounding clang.

Inside gate

An apparition emerges out of a dark cave. This is quite startling; I have no recollection of either a cave or such a being. There is a kind of shimmer around the apparition and cave alike, and although perhaps I should feel frightened by them they do seem benevolent.

- Welcome, seeker, the apparition says, I am the guardian and preserver of Gilleholmen. May I ask why you are here?

I feel I should know the answer to that but I don't. The apparition, or guardian, or whatever, senses my discomfort.

- No matter. You will find out before long. When you are ready to leave, your goal is to return to this place. I shall be waiting for you here (he gestures to the cave) but as you can see you will first need to be properly enlightened.

The apparition starts to go down into the cave but suddenly remembers something.
- Oh, silly of me, you should of course get the same as everybody else.
A small pamphlet is taken from a stack and pressed into my hand.
- Keep this. It is not strictly necessary but might be of assistance. All the very best of luck!

And the apparition disappears down into the cave.

I pocket the pamphlet. I can read it anytime I want.

I am inside of a locked gate. I could follow the apparition down into the cave but it looks dark in there. The road from outside the gate continues to the east, where I can see the mound rising.
> east

> east
I continue the road to the east and arrive at a fork.

West of mound

Here the road to the west from the gate splits into one branch going northeast and another southeast, curving around a large mound to the east. In other directions the vegetation is compact. Of course I could try to go up the mound but it seems hard. There are pine cones littered on the ground to the southeast.
> northeast

> northeast

North of mound

 The road continues. I can go east where I glimpse the upper stories of a house, or west towards the gate.  The big mound is to the south, I won't even attempt to climb it.
> east

> east

Front of house

  This is where we live in the summer! I stand in the gravel-yard in front of our big two-story house. The roads from here lead back towards the gate northwest and southwest and curve around the great mound directly to the west. I can also go around the house north or south. The big front door is closed, I think it can be opened from the inside.
> north

> north

North of house

The gravel yard continues from the front of the house to the north side where the kitchen is. A small flight of stairs leads up to the closed kitchen door. I can go west to the front of the house, or east to the seaside. To the north there is a small clearing. There is a trash can here just outside the door.
> open trash can

> open trash can
I open it. Ouch! that smell!
In it I can see a newspaper and a tangle of sticks and string.
> take tangle

> take tangle
I take it. Look, it is a broken kite!
> east

> east

Seaside of house

I have now come round the house to the seaside. It has many windows, and a balcony on the upper floor. There is a large stone terrace in front of the house, with some chairs and a table. A faint rustling suggests that some paper nearby flutters in the mild northern breeze. From an overhead tree branch hangs a large bell. There is a large double door leading into the living room but it is crawling with big ants.

The view towards the sea is spectacular. Broad stone stairs lead down to the beach. The sun makes everything almost too bright.

I can go back around the house south or north, or go down the stairs, or enter the house through the large double door.
> put kite on table

> put kite on table
I put it there.
> look under table

> look under table
Under it there is a brown mass of something. On a closer look it must be some kind of discarded wrapping paper. It must have blown there: no one would leave it like this. I pull it out so I can look at it more closely.
> north

> north

North of house

North of house outside the kitchen. I can go west to the front of the house, east to the seaside, and north to a clearing. The kitchen door is closed. There is a trash can here just outside the door.
> north

> north

Clearing

I arrive at a clearing. The ground is covered by pleasant soft grass. The clearing appears to be well used, not least by the kids, and I have very fond memories of it. Many a lazy summer day I spend here with the kids, or alone reading.
At the boundary to the north there is a swing hanging from a tree that is bent to grow almost parallel to the ground. Seaside is to the east but the view is completely blocked by dense vegetation. At the other end there is a playhouse, I cannot remember we played in it much but it was good for storing things.
The wide path to the south through a copse of trees leads back to the house.
In the middle of the clearing there is a small cart.
> in

> in

Playhouse

There is a slightly damp smell in here and it is in fact even smaller than I remember it. Various toys are scattered about. On the floor is a crumpled carpet. On one of the walls someone - can it be me? Surely not! - has made a drawing with some writing on top. There is a window looking back towards the clearing.
> look under carpet

> look under carpet
As I look under the carpet I see my moccasins under it. My moccasins! Now I can walk around without fear of nasty pine cones.
> take moccasins

> take moccasins
I put them on. Wonderful, I now can walk over pine cones.
> out

> out

Clearing

I am in the clearing with soft grass. There is a swing hanging from a tree, a playhouse, and the way back to the house is south.
In the middle of the clearing there is a small cart.
> sit on swing

> sit on swing
I am really to old for this but OK. I sit on the swing and go back and forth. This is really fun, I didn't think I would like it.

Look!! The kids come here! And we play around in the clearing, chasing each other. The smallest kid gets to ride in the cart. For some time I am that kid, understanding nothing except that the ride is hilarious and I am with friends.

The scene changes and I am somewhat older. The kids challenge me: Climb the tree to the swing, they say. I  am not sure I want to but it looks fun and I think I can do it. You are at 95 points, they say. Only five more to reach a hundred and you know what happens then. I am not certain what, but I sure want those five points. I start climbing the tree.

The tree grows at an angle and at first it is easy enough to just walk up, with the aid of some branches. Higher up it gets more level and narrow and it is hard to keep my balance.

Just before I reach the swing I lose my footing and tumble down. It is a hard landing leaving me sprawled on the ground. Oh no, the kids shout, you failed. And the landing was bad. We must detract from your points. They confer briefly and conclude: You are now at 50 points. But don't worry, there will be plenty of other ways for you to earn points.

The kids disperse. I have a strong feeling I will never reach 100 points. But from the ground I notice that there is a narrow path from the swing to the north leading into the woods.
> north

> north

Sparse wood

This is a sparse forest with a variety of trees and bushes covering the ground. The air is quite still and I cannot hear any sounds. I come here often and it is easy to navigate: to the south the path goes back to the clearing, and it continues north where the trees are fewer, and I imagine the sea is that way. The path also branches off to the west where the trees are much more dense.
> north

> north

Outside sauna

I am at the north shore. Here the water is extremely shallow and it is almost as if one could wade all the way to the next peninsula, Revsudden, though I happen to know this is not quite true. Revsudden is easy to reach by rowing and there is a stand there selling ice cream! There is no boat here now though - and I don't feel like wading.
Next to me is the sauna: a small wooden shack with a stove.
The path back to the south leads back towards the clearing. I can also go into the sauna, but it is probably not working now.
> in

> in
I go in the sauna.

In the sauna

The sauna is quite a bit smaller than I recall. It is damp and dark and there is no trace of the stove. A small window looks out on the sea. There is nothing here except for the built-in wooden bench. The only way out is where I came in.
> sit on bench

> sit on bench
I sit down on the bench and feel a bit drowsy.

Now the air gets warmer - it is really hot in here. And the kids around me are as always hilarious. We start up our traditional sauna song, about a friend of the grown-ups who accidentally brushed the hot stove and was chided by his wife.

Poor old Lars on Ekerum
Burnt a mighty blister.
Inga said you are so dumb,
Better burn you sister.
Poor old Lars on Ekerum,
Lars on Ekerum is dumb on E-E-Ekerum.

And then the kids are gone and I am left in a sauna that is far too cold. I quickly stand up. That song. Great fun but somehow does not make me proud.
> out

> out

Outside sauna

I am at the north shore. Here the water is extremely shallow and it is almost as if one could wade all the way to the next peninsula, Revsudden, though I happen to know this is not quite true. Revsudden is easy to reach by rowing and there is a stand there selling ice cream! There is no boat here now though - and I don't feel like wading.
Next to me is the sauna: a small wooden shack with a stove.
The path back to the south leads back towards the clearing. I can also go into the sauna, but it is probably not working now.
> south

> south

Sparse wood

This is a sparse forest with a variety of trees and bushes covering the ground. To the south the path goes back to the clearing, and it continues north where the trees are fewer, and I imagine the sea is that way. The path also branches off to the west where the trees are much more dense.
> west

> west

By dense forest

 Here the path ends. I am in front of a dark and forbidding forest. I can go back towards the more pleasant sparse wood to the east. If I continue west I will enter a very dense forest without any paths. I could do it but am scared: I have a feeling something bad might happen and of not finding my way out of it.
> west

> west
With trepidation I start into the forest. I climb over, crawl under, and squeeze through at various places and quickly lose all sense of direction. From the sun in the sky I can infer compass directions, but I don't know where I am and there are no paths.

Dense forest

I am in a dense forest and probably completely lost. All directions look the same.
There is a big tree here that looks climbable.
> northeast

> northeast
I walk over grass up to my knees. The vegetation around here looks menacing. I think I saw some of it before but am not sure.  I am more than a bit uneasy.

Dense forest

I am in a dense forest and probably completely lost. All directions look the same.
> west

> west
There are some boulders in my way and I sidestep them, getting deeper into the forest. The vegetation reminds me of something but I cannot say what. OK I admit I am scared, I might never find my way out.

Dense forest

I am in a dense forest and probably completely lost. All directions look the same.
> south

> south
As I force my way without much hope I suddenly see a bright light between the trees. I hurry there and finally I am out of the woods.

Sand pit

 I am almost blinded by the light after all that time in the forest. When my eyes adjust I see that I am in the place we call the 'sand pit'. It is actually a misnomer, there is no pit, just a patch of level ground where all vegetation has eroded, filled with white fine sand. We come here a lot on chilly days to play. The sun shines more brightly here than anywhere else and it is quite hot on the dry sand. The dense forest looms nearby and I should not attempt it, but there is a small breach in the vegetation to the south.
On the sand there is a spade.
> take spade

> take spade
I pick it up.
> south

> south
 I fight my way through a gap in the vegetation, leaving a hole in it where I can return whenever I want.

North of mound

I can go east where I glimpse the upper stories of a house, or west towards the gate.  There is also a recent hole in the vegetation to the north, towards the sand pit.
> east

> east

Front of house

  In front of the house. Roads curve around the mound northwest and southwest towards the gate, and I can go around the house north or south. The front door of the house is closed.
> north

> north

North of house

North of house outside the kitchen. I can go west to the front of the house, east to the seaside, and north to a clearing. The kitchen door is closed. There is a trash can here just outside the door.
> east

> east

Seaside of house

Seaside of house, there is a large stone terrace with some chairs and a table with a broken kite on. The house has many windows, and a balcony on the upper floor. From an overhead tree branch hangs a large bell. I can go around the house south or north, or down the stairs towards the sea. There is a large double door leading into the living room but it is crawling with big ants.
On the terrace there is some paper.
> down

> down

Stairs

 - Whatever happens to you, remember how beautiful this view is, grandma says as we sit on the stone stairs leading down to the sea. The pink sky of sunset hovers over the sea and colours every nook and corner of the house and grounds. I make an effort to remember. Whatever happens, I shall not forget this.
Was that just now or long ago?

The broad stairs connect the house with the sea. Close to the house they are made of solid stone blocks, further down they are a bit smaller and made of concrete. I can go up to the house or down to the foot of a long pier. I can also go south to the majestic vista that projects high over the sea.
> down

> down

Foot of pier

Here the long pier runs from the beach out into the sea. I can go out on it, or up the stairs toward the house. On either side (north and south) are small beaches I can try. The vista looms nearby but there is no direct path to it from here, I must go up the stairs a bit first.
> south

> south

Sandy beach

The beach here is of fine wet sand and it is very tempting to swim. The vista looms over us, and there is an old tub here. We used to play with it in the water, but I don't know if it is big enough to hold me now. I can go north towards the pier.
> dig

> dig
This is fun! I set out to construct sand castles with moats and towers. Suddenly I see something red in the sand. As I dig it out my castles perish, and I uncover a red bucket.
> drop spade

> drop spade
I drop it.
> enter tub

> enter tub
With great difficulty I climb into the tub. Apparently I am smaller than I thought because I fit comfortably.

And as I doze for a bit the kids come. They rush forward and start to play with me. They drag the tub with me in out into the water and turn me around. I almost lose my balance but manage to stay afloat, and eventually they drag me back onto the beach.

This was fun, though I shake a bit when climbing out of the tub. Then the kids rush away to play at other things.
> north

> north

Foot of pier

Here the long pier runs from the beach out into the sea. I can go out on it, or up the stairs toward the house. On either side (north and south) are small beaches I can try.
> north

> north

Rocky beach

The beach here is full of rocks, making it unpleasant to go swimming. There are several large rocks at the edge of the water that we use for climbing competitions. These rocks have names! In particular the largest rock is hard to climb.
I can go south towards the pier.
> climb rock

> climb rock
With difficulty I manage to climb it.

The kids appear as out of nowhere! We challenge each other to climb the rocks. I have to yield my position eventually to other kids. There is some confusion as several try to climb the same rock at the same time.

Then the kids then disappear out on the pier, shouting that they want to go for a swim.

I could follow them!
> south

> south

Foot of pier

Here the long pier runs from the beach out into the sea. I can go out on it, or up the stairs toward the house. On either side (north and south) are small beaches I can try.
> out

> out
I think I can go out on the pier. I heard the kids are out there swimming.

On the pier

The pier is very long and now I am in the middle of it. There is water on both sides. I can go out towards the sea or in towards the house.
> out

> out

End of pier

This is as far as I can get without swimming. At the end of the pier I have a fine view of the sea, and if I turn towards land I can still see the house and the vista. The kids are swimming here, having a lot of fun in the water, and want me to join!
The way from here is in towards land. There is a small boat tied to the pier.
> enter boat

> enter boat
I carefully step down into the boat. It rocks a little as I settle on one of the wooden thwarts.

Boat

The boat is nice! There is a pair of oars secured at the bottom of the boat.
> take oars

> take oars
I try to dislodge the oars but they are too heavy.
Under them I see a discarded fishing line.
> take fishing line

> take fishing line
I take it.
> out

> out

End of pier

Pier end. The kids are swimming here, having a lot of fun in the water, and want me to join!
The way from here is in towards land. There is a small boat tied to the pier.
> in

> in

On the pier

The pier is very long and now I am in the middle of it. There is water on both sides. I can go out towards the sea or in towards the house.
> in

> in

Foot of pier

Here the long pier runs from the beach out into the sea. I can go out on it, or up the stairs toward the house. On either side (north and south) are small beaches I can try.
> up

> up

Stairs

The broad stairs connect the house with the sea. Close to the house they are made of solid stone blocks, further down they are a bit smaller and made of concrete. I can go up to the house or down to the foot of a long pier. I can also go south to the majestic vista that projects high over the sea.
