Stairs

The broad stairs connect the house with the sea. Close to the house they are made of solid stone blocks, further down they are a bit smaller and made of concrete. I can go up to the house or down to the foot of a long pier. I can also go south to the majestic vista that projects high over the sea.
> south

> south

Vista

The wind here is quite strong. There is a splendid view of the sea but the impression is marred by the fact that the ground is covered with high weeds, to the extent that I cannot walk around here. The Vista is surrounded by a high but climbable parapet to keep us kids from falling off.  Right now I cannot reach it on account of the weeds. To the north I can get back to the stairs.
> cut weeds with weed cutter

> cut weeds with weed cutter
I set out to cut some weeds to get a path to the parapet at the front. This is hard work. I hope someone will appreciate it. After a while I have to rest.
> cut weeds with weed cutter

> cut weeds with weed cutter
I continue to cut weeds. This is exhausting and boring and I would be quite sweaty if it weren't for the strong wind here. I am now a significant way towards the parapet and need to take a rest.
> cut weeds with weed cutter

> cut weeds with weed cutter
Let me catch my breath. There. OK, I'm at it again. In a final spurt of energy I finally cut my way through to the parapet. The view is stunning, but I never want to cut any weeds again ever.
> drop weed cutter

> drop weed cutter
I drop it.
> north

> north

Stairs

The broad stairs connect the house with the sea. Close to the house they are made of solid stone blocks, further down they are a bit smaller and made of concrete. I can go up to the house or down to the foot of a long pier. I can also go south to the majestic vista that projects high over the sea.
> up

> up

Seaside of house

Seaside of house, there is a large stone terrace with some chairs and a table with a broken kite on. The house has many windows, and a balcony on the upper floor. From an overhead tree branch hangs a large bell. I can go around the house south or north, or down the stairs towards the sea. There is a large double door leading into the living room but it is crawling with big ants.
On the terrace there is some paper.
> take paper

> take paper
I pick it up.
> mend kite with paper

> mend kite with paper
I get to work replacing the broken paper with pieces from the wrapping paper. It is not easy and takes some time but eventually I make it. There! Now there is a proper kite!
> tie fishing line to kite

> tie fishing line to kite
I carefully tie one end of the line to the kite. Now I have a fully functional kite, and I really long to fly it.
> take kite

> take kite
I pick it up.
> down

> down

Stairs

The broad stairs connect the house with the sea. Close to the house they are made of solid stone blocks, further down they are a bit smaller and made of concrete. I can go up to the house or down to the foot of a long pier. I can also go south to the majestic vista that projects high over the sea.
> south

> south

Vista

The wind here is quite strong. There is a splendid view of the sea but the impression is marred by the fact that the ground is covered with high weeds, to the extent that I cannot walk around here. The Vista is surrounded by a high but climbable parapet to keep us kids from falling off.  A path has been cut through the weeds all the way there. To the north I can get back to the stairs.
Beside me there is a weed cutter.
> put kite on parapet

> put kite on parapet
I put it there.
> climb parapet

> climb parapet
With great difficulty I manage to climb up onto the parapet.

Parapet

From here the view is absolutely magnificent. The sea sparkles with the sun and most of Gilleholmen's seaside is laid out before me. I cannot see any boats or other people. The wind blows very strong indeed here!
At my side there is a kite.
Down below at the vista and just out of reach is a weed cutter.
> take kite

> take kite
I pick it up.
> fly kite

> fly kite
The wind catches the kite and I reel out the line. My beautiful kite rises higher and higher, pulling the line strongly, but I am rock solid now. Nothing can move me. It is just me and my kite. Nothing else matters. Time doesn't matter. As I tug the line I get the kite to fly even higher.

The kite flies. The world flies. I fly. Time flies.

This should never end. It cannot end since time has stopped. It is just me and my kite, connected by the line, and I doubt that anything else exists.

But then I hear, distantly but clearly:

GONG

The meal gong sounds. Lunch is ready. I am supposed to be there!

I try to reel in my kite but the wind is too strong. I try to climb down while holding it but I cannot. There is nothing to tie the line to here. Yet I must obey the meal gong. What should I do?

> i must let it go
> i must let it go

You think? This is not a game. The kite is life. I cannot leave or abandon it. But I must, if I want to come to lunch. The choice is impossible yet I have to do something. What?

> let it go
> let it go

I violent gust of wind decides it for me. I could not bring myself to do it but the line is whipped from my hand. Or did I actually release it myself? The kite is quickly blown away out of sight. I don't know if I will ever see it again. As I scamper down the parapet I feel like water: anything can happen to me and leave an imprint for a short while.

I run quickly over the vista and up the stairs to the house. Of the ants there is now no trace. I sit down on my place in the dining room just as the food is served. No one notices that I am upset. Or perhaps they do and don't care.
 I overhear some fragments of conversations between grown-ups:

- Did you hear from Lars yet?
- Yes they will come this weekend.
---
- What did you do with it?
- I put it on the top shelf. The kids canot reach it.
- Better lock it up, that stuff is dangerous.
---
- Any plans to go fishing this afternoon?
- I'd love to but I have to tend the lawn.

And more stuff like that.

After the meal the grownups leave to do their own things, and us kids clear the table and carry the dishes to the kitchen. I return to the dining room to check that we did not forget anything.

Dining room

In the dining room there is a table big enough to seat all of us, with lots of chairs around. I can go south to the living room, or west towards the kitchen.
> west

> west

Breakfast room

This is a small room connecting the dining room with the kitchen. Us kids have breakfast here every morning. There is a table with some chairs, and a wall covered with cupboards to hold plates and cups and stuff. There is a lot of them since grandma often hosts quite big events.
I can go east to the dining room or west to the kitchen.
> west

> west

Kitchen

The large kitchen some say is the heart of the house but I don't know if that is true. Here there are fairly standard kitchen utensils and equipment, notably a stove and a sink full of dirty dishes from the lunch and a fridge.
I would swear there should be a percolator, but I don't see it. In the corner there is a big walk-in pantry.
I can go east to the breakfast room, or out the kitchen door to the outside, or try to go in the pantry (direction: in).

> wash dishes

> wash dishes
Yes, it is time to do the dishes.

Us kids do the dishes together. It is not a chore, it is great fun! We quickly divide the work: one of us scrubs, one rinses, one dries off, and we all take turns. All along we sing our washing up song:

I am so happy washing up,
I wash up where I go.
And if I cannot wash up then
I wash up even so.

We finish in no time and quickly stove away the cleaned dishes in the cupboards in the breakfast room.

In one cupboard I notice what I did not see before: a small key hangs on the inside of one of the doors.

Breakfast room

This is a small room connecting the dining room with the kitchen. Us kids have breakfast here every morning. There is a table with some chairs, and a wall covered with cupboards to hold plates and cups and stuff. There is a lot of them since grandma often hosts quite big events.
I can go east to the dining room or west to the kitchen.
> east

> east

Dining room

In the dining room there is a table big enough to seat all of us, with lots of chairs around. I can go south to the living room, or west towards the kitchen.
> south

> south

Living room

The large living room has many pieces of comfortable furniture, notably a big armchair next to the double door leading outside. There is an old black and white TV set where we can watch children programmes in the evenings. I feel a pang of loneliness, this room should be full of people. I can go out the door, or north to the dining room, or west into the house.
> west

> west

Office

It is kind of strange to have an office as a room you have to pass through often, but perhaps not much business is conducted here nowadays. There is a big desk here. On it there are some fishing lures: colourful things with nasty hooks on them. I can go east to the living room, or west to the hall through a door that is currently closed. I can also go up to the upper floor and down into the basement.
> up

> up

Common room

I come up the stairs to the common room. The kids are all there, playing som kind of guessing game involving friends and relatives.

- Name one of my aunts, one says.
- Babo
- Correct
They laugh and turn to me.
- Who is Lars' wife?

And I know I must answer correctly or they will never let me in.
[Type the answer]
> inga

 > inga

- Yes, that's it, and all laugh in a friendly way.

The game continues for a while. I don't have to answer any more questions. Then the kids all run down the stairs.

This large and airy room has a table, a sofa and some chairs, but above all a good deal of free floor space. Us kids hang here a lot but now I am all alone. On the wall there is an old map of Gilleholmen and the surrounding small islands.
To the north are several bedrooms, but only one that I would be comfortable to enter: mine. I can go east to the seaside of the room, where there is a big balcony, or to the south where there is a corridor with more rooms. Of course I can also go back down to the office.
> north

> north

My bedroom

This is a quite small bedroom and it is currently mine. Sometimes I get another room, depending on who are here and who I can bunk with. Right now I have this single room. I don't mind being lonely, and it is kind of peaceful. Particularly on rainy days it is nice with a place to hole up in.
There is a narrow bed by the window, and not much other furniture. A bedside table with a lamp, and a small chair on which someone left a pair of socks.  I can go back out south to the common room.
> lie on bed

> lie on bed
No harm in getting into my own bed!

I start to doze and realise that I am holding a book in my hand. That book! It saved me during a rainy summer, I could always return to it and it is very long and quite exciting.

Suddenly a big ant drops from the ceiling and lands smack on the book. I jump out of the bed and throw away the book in horror. The ant gets away before I can swipe at it.

What a memory. It must be very real because now there is a book here on the floor.
> take book

> take book
I pick it up.
