Brewery

This is where grandpa had his own brewery. There are lots of vats and mysterious looking objects, in particular a machine in the corner, and pipes going here and there. It is easy to imagine the room full of activity, but it has been unused for many years. Us kids sometimes play in here. The way back to the hallway is to the east.

> x machine

> x machine
The machine rises from the floor. It has a cradle in the middle and above it there is a part with a large crank that looks as if it could be turned with some effort.
There is a small bottle fixed in the cradle.
> turn crank

> turn crank
I heave on the crank. A piston emerges from the upper part of the machine and descends about halfway to the small bottle.
> turn crank

> turn crank
I heave on the crank again. The piston descends further to touch the bottle.
> turn crank

> turn crank
Only a light touch on the crank is required, and then the piston releases and goes all the way up, its job finished for now. The cradle rotates to an unseen part of the machine and the bottle vanishes there with a loud clunk. The cradle then rotates back to its original position. It is now empty.
> put big bottle in machine

> put big bottle in machine
The big bottle snaps into the cradle with a sharp sound and remains fixed there.
> turn crank

> turn crank
I heave on the crank. The piston descends to touch the big bottle. Can it really go further? It seems dangerous to try.
> turn crank

> turn crank
I heave on the crank. It seems completely stuck. But OK, I put all my might onto this and finally something happens.
The piston drives through the neck of the bottle. The bottle explodes in thousands of small glass shards that rains over the machine and floor. Somehow my effort also bent the crank and the machine now appears unusable.

Now I have really done it. We will never be allowed in here to play again. Why did I have to do this?

In the now damaged cradle I note that the bottom of the bottle appears intact.
