The Cabin

We call this room in the basement 'the cabin', since it is made to look like a cabin on a large ship. The air is humid, as if the ventilation does not work properly, and there is a slightly mouldy smell. In his days grandpa used to house big parties here. We seldom come down here to play, the place is a bit spooky.
This is a big windowless room with (fake) round portholes and very sturdy brown furniture in the form of a sofa, some armchairs, a big table and a desk. The stairs leads back up to the office.
At the far end there is some kind of trap door but it is closed and crawling with ants. Oh no, not again! I really don't want to be here.
> open can

> open can
I pry it open. It is full of white powder, probably a very potent pesticide. I must be extremely careful not to spill any.
> pour pesticide on trap door

> pour pesticide on trap door

This will be one of the most satisfying moments of my short life.
----------------

Overcoming my fear and with supreme confidence I advance on the ants. They don't notice me. And why should they? Then I start pouring the contents of the can. A fine white powder falls on the trap door and neighbouring floor. I wonder what the ants think at first. Perhaps they think it is snowing. Probably not, they will never have experienced snow.

The effect is not immediate. The ants continue their business of crawling here and there, out of and into cracks. But after a while they start to slow down. I feel a grim satisfaction. I don't wonder what the ants feel. Perhaps for them it is only like going to sleep. If ants ever sleep.

I am like a granite cliff face, nothing can stick to me. Time passes in a strange way. One moment the ants are moving, the next moment they are all very still. How long did that really take? Did any survive? I better wait some more to be sure.

Certainly I never want to be an ant. To have my nemesis descend and in an instant - or perhaps in prolonged agony - obliterate my existence and all of those near me. It is not as if they misbehaved or did anything wrong. They just did what ants always do. Still I do not feel the slightest remorse. The only good ants are dead ants. Here there are now only good ants.
----------------

I drop the can and it rolls away into some hidden corner. I shall not need it again.
