West of mound

Here the road to the west from the gate splits into one branch going northeast and another southeast, curving around a large mound to the east. In other directions the vegetation is compact. Of course I could try to go up the mound but it seems hard. There are pine cones littered on the ground to the southeast.
> west

> west

Inside gate

I am inside of a locked gate. I could join the apparition down in the cave if I dare. The road from outside the gate continues to the east, where I can see the mound rising.
> down

> down
I climb down the cave.

Cave by the gate

My torch lights up the cave. It is not very big and even appears a cozy place. I could imagine staying here. The walls are covered with hangings and the floor with thick carpets. From a bed at the other end of the cave the so called guardian rises and greets me.
- So, you found your way at last. I hope you enjoyed your stay, he says and takes my torch to fix it to the wall.
- I did, thank you very much, and I look forward to spending more time here.
- You do? Why so? Who do you think you are?

And now I notice that we are not alone. Grandma is here with all the kids. They are unnaturally still and silent, staring at me with open eyes. They want to make sure I do this right. I shall not let them down.

- I am the next guardian. I am the preserver. Gilleholmen in this incarnation exists because of me!

Grandma and the kids avert their eyes and fade away, until only their disappointed expressions remain. The apparition looks dumbfounded and then bursts into laughter. - Really? You must have played too much Zork in your youth. No, I am the only guardian and will always be the preserver here. But who are you? What is it you should do? Have you understood this?

[type your reply]
> I remember

> I remember

The guardian nods at me. Grandma smiles benevolently and the kids look relieved.
- You understand, the guardian says, that this is important. So few remember and all this cannot be lost.
I bow my head. - I understand, I say.

We all exit the cave. The guardian starts to open the gate. As soon as it is big enough I squeeze through, not waiting for it to open fully. Behind the gate I can hear a distant relaxed chatter between grandma and the kids. It is apparently time for dinner soon. They will enjoy it without me.

The fields around the road look nice, the cows look friendly, and I am no longer very afraid of ants. The sun is way past noon, but it is still a long time to nightfall. I start going back to where I first came from.

As I walk everything around me start to dissolve. I am no longer here. For a moment it feels like I am just some text on a computer screen. My final consciousness centres on one important thought, and I manage to hold on to it for a little while before all goes blank:

I remember.

And now, gentle player, so do you.

THE END

Gilleholmen
for textadventures/quest
Joachim Parrow, April 2026

